A||N||20||Monkeyman||Tranny shifter question||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||09-27-2000||07:02 PM||||Will one of those old push button auto tranny shifters work on a Fiero??  Just wondering as I'm trying to design a custom dash for the Grape Ape.  I'd like to angle the section that used to be for the stereo and HVAC a little more than is possible with the stock shifter.<BR>If this is possible, does anyone know what cars came with a push button shifter?  Gotta know where to look for one.<P>TIA<BR><P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-27-2000||09:01 PM||||Depends on the PB shifter you want to use I guess. You want it to shift the tranny and have some type of automated force for Park like the existing park interlock to the ignition. Doesn't have to be interlocked to the switch like the OE shifter but it does need to make sure the car is in park before you exit.<P>You can raise the stock shifter by making a new bracket for it. You have to watch out for the park interlock cable on the automatic. It's not very long. You really don't want to leave an automatic w/o that interlock. No matter how much you think you'll always put it in park there will be days you will forget it. It takes very little to make the car roll away. <P>My interlock cable's head was broken for a few weaks because I lost a part of it working on the steering column and had to find a new one. It's really easy to forget to shove it in park Especially if you are used to floor shift being a manual tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||09-29-2000||02:02 AM||||Is the interlock cable the one that attaches to the front of the shifter assembly?  What happens if I remove that?  (I don't plan to, but I need to know if I can still shift out of Park.)  Since the shifter assembly just pushes/pulls that rod (that goes back to the engine), how would one of those push button shifters work?<BR>I still need to know what cars they were in so I can try to make something work.  I thought I had my custom dash planned, but I'd really like more angle to the front of the console and I just can't do that with the stock shifter in place.  (And before anyone asks, I have no intention of going to a column shifter <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  Those are fine for Grannys grocery getter, but not my Fiero.)||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-29-2000||06:16 AM||||The only one of those I've ever seen was in the mid 60's in a Valiant. God knows how it worked.<P>The interlock comes off the front of the shifter. You can still shift with the interlock disconnected but you might have to fool with it some. I Think disconnecting the shifter end of the cable leaves the interlock disabled at the ignition switch but I'm not sure.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||09-29-2000||05:22 AM||||At 1st I thought that maybe the push button shifter was electrical (obviously not 100% mechanical) but I'm starting to think that the '60s were a little too early for electrical gadgets like that.  Wasn't almost everything run off of vacuum back then?  If so, this might prove unreliable.  <P>Would anything that had the capability of pushing/pulling the shifter rod work?  Would a cable operated system work as well?  (I have to get something angled.)||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-29-2000||09:42 AM||||Like I said. I've only seen that one PB system. I've never seen anything else but race systems and those are custom built transmissions that shift electrically.<P><BR>cables don't care about angle. they only <BR>care that you don't bend them too tight. <P>You could mount the shifter upside down on the roof and they wouldn't care as long as they reach and aren't bent at sharp angles.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||09-29-2000||06:49 PM||||I spoke with a friend today who was under the impression that the pushbutton shifter (early to mid-60s Barracudas and Valiants) would only work for certain trannys.  Bad for me.  Can you suggest any options?  I don't have a lot of money to spend, but I'd sure like to find something to replace the original shifter.  Gotta get that out of the way.  Once I get this figured out, I can actually get to work on the dash.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||09-29-2000||07:21 PM||||could the shifter cables be modified, so a new shifter could be relocated further back in the console?<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000008||Monkeyman||09-30-2000||05:23 AM||||I thought about that, Steve.  The only problem is that the shifter is in a "well".  The "hump" rises considerably just aft of the shifter.  If I could move the shifter back about 6", I'd be fine.  Good suggestion, though.||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||09-30-2000||09:50 AM||||If you have the Grand Am brakes, you should figure out a way to use your E-brake handle as your auto shifter. On the surface, both seem to operate in the same manner, but what the hell do I know.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||09-30-2000||02:39 PM||||Now that would be trick! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I don't see a problem with setting up some kind of cable operated linkage, but wouldn't I need something so that I know which gear I'm in?  And what about something to keep me from accidentally shifting into reverse at speed?  Now I'm getting excited.  Couldn't get much more cool than that.<P>Now for reality.  Who sees any problems with this idea?  Any way of making some kind of detent/reverse lockout thing?  C'mon, guys.  I need your brilliance more than ever. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||StuGood||09-30-2000||06:31 PM||||How about an hydraulic shifter?  That way, you can join in the fun of bleeding the cylinders (one push, one pull), like us manual-shift guys  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !!  Heeheehee Sick...<P>Okay.  You asked for this, so here's my take:<P>Could you leave the whole shifter mechanism where it is, interlock and all, and operate the existing stick (chopped, if necessary) with a slider that sticks down through a slot in your new console?  You'd have mount the slider onto some decent ways (or bearings) or something, but I think you get the idea.  The slider would slide right up and down along your new ramped console.    <P>The 2nd most obvious thing to me, is see if you can just remount the existing shifter mechanism at an angle, to match the slope of your new console.  Don't know if this is feasible, or maybe wouldn't give the look you want to achieve.  But it could save a lot of headaches, using the existing mechanism.  Consider shortening the stick, too, if that would help it look right.<P>What about the interlock cable?  If you can't make it work due to relocating the shifter, you might consider bypassing the interlock and putting a "park" switch in there that fools the existing blue "dingy box" into thinking you left your keys in, if you start to get out w/o putting it in park, and give you a buzz.  Not as goof-proof as the interlock, of course.<P>By the way, FWIW some of the Corvairs had a little tiny sliding shifter on the dash, for automatic trans.  But that was a Powerglide -no idea if it really could be made to work.<P>'Nuff fer now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> . <BR>||||reg||12
Z||000012||Monkeyman||09-30-2000||07:29 PM||||The stock shifter wouldn't give me the look I'm after, but those are some good ideas.  I've been thinking about the Ebrake/shifter thing, though.  1st, I'd just about <I>have</I> to be in Park because I couldn't get out if I wasn't.  And second, it would make a heck of a theft deterrent.  Who's gonna drive the thing if they can't figure out how to put it in gear? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  No one (except for the 1300 of you) would ever think to operate the Ebrake handle.  Sir Ogre already said that a cable operated system would work, so all I would need to devise would be a detent system so I know what gear I'm in.<P>Question:  Which end of the system tells me what gear I'm in?  Is it the detents on the shifter end, or is it something on the tranny end?  If I didn't have a detent bracket (don't know what it's called--the piece of metal that has the notches that goes on the bottom of the shifter), would the shifter lever still know where to stop for each gear?  Is the detent bracket thing the only thing that keeps me from accidently shifting into Reverse?<P>Sorry for the bandwidth, but this is getting interesting.  I've never been good at actually finishing a project and I'd like to finish this one.||||reg||1
Z||000013||deceler8||10-01-2000||09:43 AM||||This will be an interesting project. I've always been under the impression that the detents are inside the trans, but don't really know for sure. I once had a car with an aftermarket auto floor shifter and this thing had no detents of its own.<P>I also have the Grand Am brakes on the rear and I was thinking about the E brake handle as a shifter for a more practical reason...freeing up the area where the current shifter resides to put a couple cupholders. But you could also put audio equipment or gauges there...even a real glovebox.<P>Sure, there are problems with this approach. The interlock issue seems to be a minor one as auto trans cars with floorshifts came without them for years. Knowing what gear you're in is a minor problem as well.<P>Finding a way to lock the handle in place or detent it is probably the biggest challenge.<P>It would be cool...like a fighter jet with a sidestick. I also recall the Corvair shifter (my parents had one of these)...it was a Powerglide but I don't recall the shifter having a Park position (just RNDL). ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||10-01-2000||10:27 AM||||Making sure that I wouldn't accidentally shift into Reverse or Park would be of utmost importance, especially with the shifter mounted down there.  I can just picture putting my hand down there to adjust myself in my seat (at 60mph) and finding myself in Park with little pieces of my tranny scattered all over the road.  As far as knowing what gear I'm in, it wouldn't matter to me.  I go to work in the dark everyday and haven't had a shifter console for about 6 weeks.  No biggie.  Like I said before, I don't mind not having a Park interlock although I agree with ogre that normally it would be important.  <P>Email me if you want to discuss this further.  I don't want to take up too much bandwidth unless this is an important issue to anyone else.||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||10-01-2000||11:10 AM||||There are 2 sets of "detents"<P>one set lines up the valve in the trans. That's all it does.<P>The other would more correctly be called a gate plate and is in the shifter. that's what keeps you from shifting to the wrong gear. The only 2 positions you are allowed w/o pushing the button are drive and neutral. All the others except park funnel into those 2. (you can ger from R to N but not the other way w/o the button.)<P>The shift interlock and gating are tied to the button. not the stick. You are going to have a very dificult time making the e brake a shifter.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||10-01-2000||11:28 AM||||Not doubting you, ogre, but assuming that I could make a gate plate with correctly positioned detents and didn't need/want the Park interlock, what would be the problems?  Here's what I've figured out (based on my limited knowledge).  I'd need a pair of cables (since I don't think you can push a cable, just pull it) to run from the tranny to the new shifter, some sort of bracket (at the shifter end) to hold the cables in place, and a gate plate with the correct detents.  Like I said, I'm not concerned with the Park interlock.  I understand your point about it, but since the shifter/E brake lever would have to be all the way down (and in Park) to exit/enter the vehicle, I think that would take care of itself.  I assume that the "button" you're referring to is the one on the shifter handle?  Wouldn't the principle be the same whether it was the stock shifter or the E brake lever?  What's the difference between the two that would make problems?  I plan on removing the E brake setup from the Monkeymobile today so I can take a better look at its guts.  Please explain the "shift interlock".  Maybe I have that confused with the "Park interlock"?||||reg||1
Z||000017||deceler8||10-01-2000||12:11 PM||||Based on my even more limited knowledge, seems like one cable will do if there is a spring at the trans end that always wants to pull the cable to a default position (whether it be Park or 1). If the handle can be locked so that it will only move when the button is pushed, maybe that will eliminate the need for a gate/detent at the shifter end.<P>If the handle will only move when the button is pushed, you can adjust whatever you want (ugh) and not have to worry about leaving parts in the road. Or use the handle as is and just get some better fitting dainties (LOL).||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||10-01-2000||12:35 PM||||I want to be able to move freely between Neutral and Drive (slick roads, skid, drop it into Neutral to get traction back).  I think I understand how the detent/gate plate at the shifter works.  The detents aren't all the same depth.  <P>(tried to make a diagram of the gate plate--didn't work--neither does my scanner)<P>Let's see if I have this right (this would, of course be curved, not flat).  I'm pretty sure this is about what the gate plate looks like.  Like Ogre said, you can slide from R into N without touching the button and back and forth between N & D.  How difficult would it be to make another gate plate to match this one but on a scale that would match that of the E brake lever?  I think that the base of the E brake uses a different kind of catch (pawl instead of pin?) but I don't see why this couldn't be modified without too much trouble.  <P>What else am I missing??<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 10-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||deceler8||10-01-2000||02:29 PM||||It also occurred to me that you could have a park interlock with the E-brake by wiring the existing brake light warning switch into the ignition switch so that the car would only start with the handle in the Park (or brake off) position.<P>You might get more ideas if you open another thread with this as the subject (E-Brake handle as trans shifter).<P>This is probably gonna be like a lot of other mods...can probably be done, but you'll have to decide how many features you wanna give up to have it.<P>Just seems like a good idea as it's already there and the lack of interior room limits your options.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Monkeyman||10-01-2000||02:59 PM||||Good idea.  Watch for a new thread. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
